# Kettlecorn use cases

```elixir
Mix.install([{:kettlecorn, "~> 0.2.0"}])
```

## About this notebook

Use a sandboxed VM to run user code, load plugins, and test submissions.
Start with [Getting started](getting-started.livemd)
and [Working with guest VMs](working-with-guests.livemd) for the APIs.

See [Network access](network.livemd) for socket and HTTP grants.

Requires Elixir 1.20 or later on x86_64 or ARM64 Linux or Apple silicon macOS.

```elixir
{:ok, runtime} = Kettlecorn.Install.runtime()
```

## Run code your users send

A playground or an online judge runs code it can't trust. Give each run a
separate VM with `Kettlecorn.run/2`, so runs can't see each other's state,
and limit each one: memory, wall-clock time for the VM and the evaluation,
and CPU (fuel):

```elixir
limits = [
  max_memory: 128 * 1024 * 1024,
  max_lifetime: 20_000,
  max_fuel: 100_000_000_000,
  timeout: 2_000
]

run = fn code ->
  case Kettlecorn.run(code, limits) do
    {:ok, %{"inspected" => result, "output" => output}} -> {:ok, result, output}
    {:error, %{"kind" => kind, "message" => message}} -> {:error, kind, message}
    {:error, reason} -> {:error, "vm_error", inspect(reason)}
  end
end

submissions = [
  ~S{Enum.sum(1..100)},
  ~S{IO.puts("printing"); :ok},
  ~S{raise ArgumentError, "bad input"},
  ~S{Stream.repeatedly(fn -> :spin end) |> Stream.run()},
  ~S{File.read!("/etc/passwd")}
]

submissions
|> Task.async_stream(run, timeout: 30_000, max_concurrency: 4)
|> Enum.zip_with(submissions, fn {:ok, result}, code -> {code, result} end)
```

The spinning submission hits its 2-second timeout and the one that reads
`/etc/passwd` finds no such file. Each submission runs in a separate VM.

To reduce startup latency for many short runs, keep a pool of VMs ready
to accept code. Assign each VM to one run and stop it afterward.

## Give the guest controlled access

Register a host call that looks up products in an inventory owned by the
host. This VM has a ten-minute lifetime. Rerun the setup cell if it expires.

```elixir
inventory = %{"apple" => %{price: 0.5, stock: 12}, "pear" => %{price: 0.75, stock: 0}}

lookup = fn
  %{"sku" => sku} when is_binary(sku) ->
    case Map.fetch(inventory, sku) do
      {:ok, item} -> {:ok, item}
      :error -> {:error, "unknown sku"}
    end

  _args ->
    {:error, "expected a SKU"}
end

{:ok, shop} =
  Kettlecorn.start_link(
    handlers: %{"lookup" => lookup},
    max_memory: 128 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_fuel: 100_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 600_000
  )
```

The guest requests three SKUs, then calls an unregistered operation.

```elixir
code = ~S"""
lookup = fn sku ->
  case Kettlecorn.Host.call("lookup", %{"sku" => sku}) do
    {:ok, %{price: price, stock: stock}} when stock > 0 -> {sku, price}
    {:ok, _out_of_stock} -> {sku, :out_of_stock}
    {:error, reason} -> {sku, reason}
  end
end

{Enum.map(["apple", "pear", "plum"], lookup), Kettlecorn.Host.call("delete_all", %{})}
"""
```

```elixir
{:ok,
 %{
   "value" => {
     [{"apple", 0.5}, {"pear", :out_of_stock}, {"plum", "unknown sku"}],
     {:error, "no host handler"}
   }
 }} = Kettlecorn.eval(shop, code)
```

```elixir
Kettlecorn.stop(shop)
```

The guest waits up to five seconds by default. If the call times out,
Kettlecorn cancels the handler's process.

## Host third-party plugins

Run a third-party pricing rule as a GenServer in its own VM. Call it with
`Kettlecorn.call/4`, which takes a timeout like `GenServer.call/3`. This
interactive VM has a ten-minute lifetime; restart this cell if it expires:

```elixir
plugin = ~S"""
defmodule Discount do
  use GenServer
  def init(rate), do: {:ok, rate}
  def handle_call({:price, amount}, _from, rate), do: {:reply, amount * (1 - rate), rate}
  def handle_call(:hang, _from, rate), do: (Process.sleep(:infinity); {:reply, :never, rate})
end

GenServer.start(Discount, 0.1, name: :discount)
"""

{:ok, rules} =
  Kettlecorn.start_link(
    max_memory: 128 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_fuel: 100_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 600_000
  )

{:ok, _} = Kettlecorn.eval(rules, plugin)

{:ok, 90.0} = Kettlecorn.call(rules, :discount, {:price, 100})
```

A call that exceeds its timeout returns `{:error, :timeout}`. Stop the
plugin's VM before replacing it:

```elixir
{:error, :timeout} = Kettlecorn.call(rules, :discount, :hang, 1_000)
Kettlecorn.stop(rules)
```

A plugin that needs Hex packages can be packaged as a Mix application with
`mix kettlecorn.cook` and started with `Kettlecorn.start_link(app: dir)`.

## Run submission tests with ExUnit

ExUnit is part of the guest's runtime. This example runs a student's module
and the grader's tests in a throwaway VM and returns the test summary.

The submission and tests share the guest VM. A submission can alter the
tests or forge the summary.

```elixir
submission = ~S"""
defmodule Fizz do
  def buzz(n) when rem(n, 15) == 0, do: "FizzBuzz"
  def buzz(n) when rem(n, 3) == 0, do: "Fizz"
  def buzz(n) when rem(n, 5) == 0, do: "Buzz"
  def buzz(n), do: n
end
"""

tests = ~S"""
ExUnit.start(autorun: false, formatters: [])

defmodule FizzTest do
  use ExUnit.Case
  test "multiples of 3", do: assert(Fizz.buzz(9) == "Fizz")
  test "multiples of 5", do: assert(Fizz.buzz(10) == "Buzz")
  test "multiples of 15", do: assert(Fizz.buzz(30) == "FizzBuzz")
  test "others", do: assert(Fizz.buzz(7) == "7")
end

ExUnit.run() |> Map.new(fn {key, count} -> {Atom.to_string(key), count} end)
"""

{:ok, %{"value" => %{"failures" => 1} = summary}} =
  Kettlecorn.run(submission <> tests,
    max_memory: 128 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_fuel: 100_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 60_000,
    timeout: 30_000
  )

summary
```

The last test fails: the submission returns `7`, not `"7"`. The summary is
the map `ExUnit.run/0` returns, with the number of tests and failures.
String keys keep the result independent of which atoms the host has loaded.

